{"term":"défloration","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"virginity_loss","concept":"defloration","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":25,"technical":70,"recognition_pct_current":80,"recognition_pct_peak":85,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The act of a woman's first penetrative vaginal intercourse, framed around rupture of the hymen and loss of virginity; historically central to honor-based assessments of female sexual purity in French legal and moral discourse.","english_term":"defloration","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"French legal tradition treated défloration simultaneously as a civil wrong and a family honor event; persists in legal history, literary, and forensic medicine contexts."}
{"term":"pudeur","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":null,"concept":"sexual_modesty_virtue","is_novel_concept":true,"register":"moral_religious","crude":0,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":92,"recognition_pct_peak":95,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"An innate, culturally valorized sense of modesty and sexual shame; the feeling of reticence or bashfulness about bodily exposure and sexuality, framed in French culture as a positive feminine virtue rather than a pathology.","english_term":"modesty","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Unlike English 'prudishness' (pejorative), pudeur is normatively positive in French moral culture; it underwrites the former legal category 'atteinte à la pudeur' and remains in everyday use without archaic or clinical register."}
{"term":"honte sexuelle","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":null,"concept":"sexual_repression","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":10,"technical":40,"recognition_pct_current":70,"recognition_pct_peak":70,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Sexual shame; the internalized feeling of guilt, disgust, or dishonor arising from sexual desire, behavior, or bodily exposure, especially when conditioned by religious or honor-culture norms.","english_term":"sexual shame","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"A compound descriptor rather than a fixed lexical term; more prevalent in psychological and feminist discourse than vernacular usage; maps imperfectly onto 'sexual repression,' which foregrounds suppression rather than shame as its mechanism."}
{"term":"salissure","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":null,"concept":"sexual_defilement","is_novel_concept":true,"register":"literary","crude":35,"technical":15,"recognition_pct_current":52,"recognition_pct_peak":68,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A stain or soiling; in sexual honor-shame contexts, the metaphorical moral pollution resulting from illicit sexual contact — particularly the contamination of female virginity or family honor through sexual transgression.","english_term":"defilement","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Primarily a physical-stain word repurposed metaphorically in Catholic and honor-culture discourse to frame sexuality as moral contamination; rarely used in modern vernacular but recurrent in 19th–20th century literary and judicial texts."}
{"term":"atteinte à la pudeur","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"indecent_assault","concept":"indecent_act","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":15,"technical":85,"recognition_pct_current":68,"recognition_pct_peak":82,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The former French legal designation for sexual assault short of rape — literally 'offense against modesty' — encompassing indecent touching, exposure, and sexual harassment; abolished from the Penal Code in 1994.","english_term":"indecent assault","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Replaced by 'agression sexuelle' in the 1994 Penal Code reform; the pudeur framing positions the offense as a violation of public decency rather than of bodily autonomy, reflecting the honor-shame moral architecture of pre-reform French law."}
{"term":"outrage aux mœurs","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":null,"concept":"indecency","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":10,"technical":80,"recognition_pct_current":58,"recognition_pct_peak":78,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A former French legal offense meaning 'outrage to public morals,' covering any publication, performance, or public conduct deemed grossly offensive to community standards of sexual decency, including pornography and public indecency.","english_term":"outrage to public decency","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Broad morality-based charge used from the Napoleonic era into the late 20th century; used to prosecute Flaubert (Madame Bovary, 1857) and Baudelaire (Les Fleurs du Mal, 1857); substantially reformed and replaced by more specific modern offenses."}
